meta {
  name: Delete Product Subpath - Deletes Entire Product (Warning)
  type: http
  seq: 13
}

delete {
  url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/REPLACE_WITH_PRODUCT_ID/categories
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  Confirmed live 2026-09-08 against two separate disposable products
  (`WC-TEST-DISPOSABLE-001` id `6aa022442b6fd055ffb7caa5`,
  `WC-TEST-DISPOSABLE-002` id `6aa0228d2b6fd055ffb7caa6`, both since
  deleted).
  
  ## Critical, potentially hazardous finding
  `DELETE /products/{identifier}/{path}` does **not** scope the delete to
  `{path}` at all -- it deletes the **entire product**, regardless of what
  `{path}` is. Confirmed two ways:
  1. `PATCH`'d `category_ids` onto a disposable product (confirmed linked
     via `GET .../categories`), then called `DELETE
     .../products/{id}/categories` expecting it to unassign just the
     category -- instead the *whole product* was deleted (`204`, then a
     plain `GET /products/{id}` returned `404`, not just an empty
     categories list).
  2. Repeated with a **completely made-up** subpath name
     (`/totally_made_up_path_xyz`) on a second disposable product with no
     special setup -- same result: `204`, whole product gone. The `{path}`
     segment is not inspected by the delete handler at all; it behaves
     identically to plain `DELETE /products/{identifier}`.
  
  **Implication for docs**: never document or suggest
  `DELETE /products/{id}/{subpath}` as a way to remove a single
  relationship/field (e.g. "unassign a category" or "remove an asset") --
  it silently deletes the whole product instead. This is exactly the kind
  of undeclared-route landmine `USER_FLOWS.md` warned could exist; flag to
  Plytix as a likely bug (a scoped-removal endpoint that isn't scoped) or,
  at minimum, a sharp edge worth a loud warning in any future docs that
  mention it.
}
